g to list the main materials and their designations: Stainless steel: It is a classic choice when it comes to the choice of a frying pan. This skillet is suitable for a wide range of cooking tasks, e.g., sautéing and frying Skillet with a non-stick coating: Thanks to this coating, such skillets are a perfect choice when you need to prepare delicate foods that often stick, for instance, fish or fried eggs. Carbon steel: Such a skillet is heavy-duty and a good choice for stovetop and grill cooking of sears, chars, and crispies. Cast iron: To a certain extent, it can fulfill the same functions as a carbon steel pan, however, one should keep in mind that it is slower during seasoning-in, it conducts the heat a bit worse, and reacts less to temperature changes. Choose the Right Skillet for Your Purpose Since you already know the difference in materials of frying pans and are already aware of how to measure a skillet size, it is highly likely that you have determined a frying pan of a certain material and size for your cooking needs. It is likely that you will need two skillets, let’s say, one 7.9” and a larger one. Probably, you will select skillets of different...
- [How to Cook with a Wok](https://us.yosukata.com/blogs/articles/how-to-cook-with-a-wok): Wok dishes are the hallmark of Chinese cuisine and Asian cuisine in general. To cook them, you need a special wok pan. This blog post will be devoted to this kitchen utility, as well as how to cook with a wok. The classic Chinese wok has a round shape, a convex bottom, high and thin walls. It is made of cast iron or carbon steel and has one or two handles. In China, they often cook on special burners, in the center of which there is a semicircular notch. With its help, the wok gains stability, and the powerful flame heats the walls quickly and evenly. For ordinary home cooking, a special household, also called European, wok with a flat bottom is manufactured. This wok is suitable for standard gas and induction cookers. These pans are generally made of carbon steel (black or blue), aluminum, or stainless steel and are seasoned with a non-stick coating. They come with one long or two short handles. What is a wok pan used for? The answer is simple. You need a wok to cook quickly. Cooking in a wok usually takes less than 15 minutes, so as a result, you will get the food that is: Useful: Food cooked in a wok turns out to be healthier than in a regular pan since the ingredients are fried for 1-3 minutes. With such a quick heat treatment, the food does not lose vitamins and nutrients. Not greasy: To fry all the ingredients of the dish, a tablespoon of oil is enough, and in cases of cooking fatty meat, you can do without oil at all. Crunchy and aromatic: Meat and vegetables, when quickly roasted, acquire a dense crispy crust and an appetizing appearance. Besides, in a wok, you can cook in a variety of ways - from stir-frying to smoking, steaming, braising, and more. After reading why so many professional and amateur chefs prefer woks to more commonplace approaches to cooking, you might have already decided to get a wok for yourself. So, keep reading to know more about the possible ways of cooking with a wok. Wok: What Are We Actually Talking about? It is believed that food cooked in a wok is distinguished by its taste and naturalness. The cooking time is much shorter, and the food retains more vitamins. However, choosing a wok is not as easy as using it. It is necessary to take into account many parameters and especially the purpose. The taste and aroma of the prepared dishes will largely depend on this. A wok is truly versatile for cooking. Wondering what to cook in a wok? You can prepare absolutely any food and even porridge. The wok can be used as a deep fat fryer. The most popular way is stir-fry: frying with constant stirring, which is suitable for cooking noodles with vegetables. However, ordinary frying is also one of the methods of cooking in this utensil. So, let us dwell in more detail on the most common ways of preparing food with the help of a wok and provide you with useful tips on how to cook with a wok. Stir-Frying It is one of the most common methods of preparing food in a wok. Well, it is quite logical since this utensil is the best option to choose for cooking dishes by the stir-frying method. This cooking technique is absolutely incompatible with large portions. If you overfill the wok, the food will be stewed rather than fried. Fast frying in a wok with constant stirring gives food a special flavor - wok hei. A dish is considered to be cooked correctly if it has this flavor. You can get it by strictly observing two conditions: by strongly heating the surface of the wok leaving enough space in the pan for stirring the ingredients. Ideally, the diameter of a stir-frying wok should not exceed 40 cm. Here are the main recommendations to follow: You need to do everything quickly. Prepare all the ingredients and the necessary equipment in advance and arrange them so that, if necessary, you can easily reach them. Dry the food and make sure there is no excess moisture on the surface. Water droplets can spoil the overall picture, preventing the formation of a golden color. For convenience, all components of the dish can be decomposed separately: the main ingredients, seasonings, and sauce. Stir-frying requires maximum preheating. The pan should literally be smoking. The oil that is resistant to high temperatures is suitable for wok. The best options are peanut and sesame oil, as well as avocado oil. You need a small amount of oil for cooking. Steaming Steamed products retain not only their natural color, smell, shape, and taste, but also most of the trace elements and vitamins contained in the raw product. The method is suitable for those who follow a strict or healthy diet. For steam cooking, it is better to use a wok with two handles so that it is convenient to place a steamer or a special grid on it. How to cook with a wok on the steam? Here are the steps Add water to the wok so that there is 2-3 cm left to the wire rack or to the bottom of the bamboo steamer. Place the products in a chaotic manner and cover the steamer with a lid, if this is a wire rack, then cover the entire wok. Steaming is best...
- [Carbon Steel: The Kitchen Investment of a Lifetime](https://us.yosukata.com/blogs/articles/carbon-steel-the-kitchen-investment-of-a-lifetime): If you’re trying to decide on cookware to invest in, look no further than carbon steel: It’s the cookware every top chef prefers, including famous American cook Julia Child. With its multipurpose functionality, one pan can easily replace most of the rest of your current set. Why clutter your cabinets with cheap alternatives when one excellent pan can do it all? Carbon steel is the one pan that can do it all. An Introduction to Carbon Steel Although the difference between cast iron and carbon steel isn’t major, professional chefs prefer carbon steel due to its ability to withstand high heat including temperatures that exceed those on your home stovetop. Although aluminum and stainless steel pans heat food quickly, they do not perform nearly as well at high temperatures. Not only do carbon steel pans withstand the high temperatures of industrial kitchen stoves, they heat up faster than their cast iron counterparts. They can also keep food piping hot in the pan when you’re fixing up those side dishes and completing other kitchen duties. In addition, carbon steel pans are more lightweight and hard-wearing and heat up faster and more evenly than cast iron. They’re also easier to maneuver and are more durable than stainless steel, copper, and nonstick pans. Although they’re not as effective as stainless steel at retaining heat off the burner, they do maintain consistent heat while you’re cooking and can withstand temps up to 1200 F. Maintenance To maintain their high performance, you will need to season your carbon steel cookware to ensure it continues to be nonstick, but compared with cast iron which may need multiple seasonings, you’ll have to do it just once. Carbon steel pans are easily cleaned with water and a sponge or a soft bristle brush and can be dried by briefly putting them back on a slow burner. Just don’t put them in the dishwasher as they can rust, and avoid cooking acidic foods such as tomatoes or citrus as they can degrade the finish. If maintained properly, carbon steel will last forever and will age gracefully. You don’t have to worry about using metal utensils as any scratches are cured with re-seasoning. Disadvantages The biggest downside to carbon steel is that it should never go into the dishwasher, but as long as you perform basic maintenance, your pan will last a lifetime in contrast to more conventional, popular cookware that you’d otherwise have to invest in year after year. Both carbon steel and cast iron pans compare to aluminum alloys are also relatively poor conductors of heat and consequently more prone to uneven heating patterns. They may be hot in the middle right over the burner but cold around the unheated perimeter. They’re also reactive metals that do not tolerate acidic or alkaline ingredients well. Blue vs. Black Steel When looking for carbon steel pans, you’ll come across both blue and black steel varieties. The colors allude to the surface-hardening treatments designed to prevent rust that make the surface of a new pan look either blue or black as opposed to silver. Black carbon steel, in particular, has a black color that comes from black iron oxide. It is relatively stable and less prone to corrosion—making them essentially invincible. Compare to carbon steel that require bluing to form protective layer, black carbon steel pans could be seasoned straight away and used for cooking. Black iron oxide layer is sensitive to acidic food and can wear off if not seasoned properly. Cast Iron vs. Carbon Steel As mentioned previously, there’s not much difference between carbon steel and cast iron in terms of retaining heat and durability. They both need proper seasoning to maintain their nonstick qualities, and both can go from the stovetop straight into the oven. Carbon steel is composed of 99 percent iron and 1 percent other minerals including carbon, whereas cast iron contains 2 to 4 percent other minerals including carbon and 96 to 98 percent iron. There are, however, minor differences. Carbon steel pans is more appealing as it’s made from sheets of metal which gives it a smoother surface that actually may be more nonstick than a seasoned cast iron pan. Differences in design also make each more suitable for different dishes. For instance, cast iron skillets have vertical sides that are better for cooking chicken, cornbread or pizza while carbon steel pans have sloping sides that are better for sautéing. Because cast iron skillets are also thicker and heavier, they can heat food a bit more evenly than carbon steel, it’s also easier to sear meats in them. Carbon steel pans have sloped sides, making them more equipped for sautéing. Carbon steel, on the other hand, is lighter, so it’s easier to lift when tossing ingredients. The grain structure, which determines how a pan performs and how it can be molded, is also a diverging factor between the two. The extra carbon in cast iron makes it more brittle as carbon tends to lump into carbides—a mixture of iron and carbon—or to form...
- [Prepare a Tasty Stir-fry in a Wok: Tips & Recipes](https://us.yosukata.com/blogs/articles/prepare-a-tasty-stir-fry-in-a-wok-tips-recipes): Stir-fry is a cooking technique and a dish name at the same time. Quickly fried vegetables, seafood, meat, nuts, tofu, and sprouts on a skillet or wok using much less oil than for similar dishes. If you are wondering how to make a stir-fry in a wok, then this article is right for you. The good news is that making stir-fry is extremely fast and simple. Ideally, healthy vegetables should remain half raw, bright in color, and slightly crunchy. Meat, seafood, or tofu should be fully cooked. Spices, sauces, nuts, other superfoods, and condiments are optional. In winter, you should not hesitate to use frozen vegetables. Because it's better to eat a cooked, defrosted serving of vitamins and fiber than not eating vegetables at all. How to Make Stir-fry: All Our Trick Cooking is an art! However, you can master it if you are ready to try again and again. Moreover, there are numerous amazing recipes that guide you step by step through all the pitfalls to achieve the best results. Yosukata has a Youtube video with stir-fry cooking tips. The same story is with stir-fry. Even though the dish is quite simple and understandable while cooking, there are also numerous issues to pay attention to in order to achieve the perfect taste. It is essential to keep the right balance of textures, flavors, and presentation. Thus, you will get the perfect dish just like out of the restaurant. Perhaps, you already know some of these tips from other recipes or from your experience. In this article, we’ve tried to gather all of them together so that you could get all the necessary information in one place and understand how to stir fry in a wok. Everything related to stir-fry is simple and you can try most recipes right away without the long preparation time. We’ll add a recipe to each section so that you can see how many options of stir-fry exist and how you can change the ingredients to create something new. If you don’t like anything in the recipes, think about what other products you can use to make it to your liking. Stir-fry can be vegetarian or vegan, you can arrange it to any preferences or restrictions. This is a perfect dish for lunch or dinner, you can take it to the office in the lunch box or heat it the next day in the microwave oven for breakfast. Overall, it is definitely a must-try! Let’s start with a quick and simple recipe: Vegetable stir-fry Quickly fried vegetables are perfect as a side dish with meat or fish or as a separate dish. Ingredients: broccoli - 1 small head of cabbage cabbage - 200 g corn in grains - 100 g vegetable broth - 50 ml soy sauce - 1 tbsp sugar - 1 tsp lemon juice - 1 tbsp vegetable oil for frying - 2-3 tbsp. How to cook: In a bowl, combine the broth, soy sauce, sugar, and lemon juice, whisk until the sugar dissolves. Disassemble the broccoli into inflorescences, finely chop the cabbage. If using frozen kale, defrost it. If canned, drain off excess juice. Heat vegetable oil in a wok. Once the oil is well heated, add the broccoli blossoms and fry, stirring occasionally, for 1-2 minutes. Then add the cabbage to the broccoli, followed by the corn. Fry the vegetables until tender. At the very end, pour the sauce into the wok and stir all the ingredients. Serve hot. Ingredients for a Stir-fry There are numerous recipes for cooking a stir-fry and new ones appear regularly. However, if you know the principles of making this dish, you’ll be able to create your own versions with your favorite products. Let’s have a look at the basic rules for cooking stir fry: 1. Protein Chicken, pork, thin beef slices, shrimp, calamari, fish fillets diced, or tofu. It is better to prepare meat and poultry in advance: cut them into small slices, chop with a fork, salt, pepper and let it stand for some time. 2. Aromatic component Onions (sweet or shallots), garlic, green onions, or ginger. 3. Vegetables On