- [Deep Frying in a Wok: What You Should Know](https://us.yosukata.com/blogs/articles/deep-frying-in-a-wok-what-you-should-know): Most people are surprised to learn that a wok is quite a multifaceted piece of cookware. It is typically associated with Asian cuisine and methods like stir-frying and steaming. In reality, you can cook an impressive array of dishes using these peculiar flare-shaped pans. They also double as bowls! Here are the fundamentals of deep frying in a wok. Using a Deep Fry Wok Your deep fry wok has many hidden talents. This cooking technique is its big strength, and you may be surprised by the sheer variety of ingredients that can be thoroughly fried in this cookware. Carbon steel woks are the best, as they perform great at intense heat. Discover the best ways to cook in a wok below. Is a Wok Good for Deep Frying? Yes, and this is an understatement! The secret is the flared shape. A wok lets you cook smaller foods quickly at intense heat. At the same time, it is a great time saver. You can fit a lot of ingredients together but cook them just as quickly. In addition, when you deep fry in a wok made of carbon steel, your meals are healthier due to the absence of chemicals on the surface. The non-stick surface is created by oil particles. A carbon steel wok is a powerful workhorse that will serve you for decades. Benefits of Deep Frying in a Wok Is a wok good for deep frying of only some ingredients? No, this cookware is very versatile. Of course, when most people think of frying, they rarely associate it with woks, but this is actually what they are best for! Discover the biggest strengths of a carbon steel wok in comparison with an ordinary frying pan, a Dutch oven, or a stockpot. 1 Flared Shape Reduces Oil Splatter Thanks to the sloping shape, deep frying in a carbon steel wok is less messy. It has high and wide sides that rise up earlier than those of its counterparts, so a lot of oil spatter remains inside. 2 Cook with Less Oil For the same reason, you can achieve the necessary oil depth with less oil. Some studies have shown that the difference is as big as 33%! 3 Maneuverability When deep frying in a carbon steel wok, you can move the ingredients more easily. You can add them, remove them, and move around inside to get an even fry. 4 Cook More and Faster The shape of a wok creates a bigger surface area in comparison with conventional pans. This lets you fit in more ingredients, but spend the same amount of time working in the kitchen. What Can I Cook? Before we explain how to deep fry in a wok, make sure you know what ingredients to pick. Woks show the most impressive results when they are used to cooking smaller things, such as chicken nuggets, calamari, or french fries. Generally, any batches of smaller items show the best results. Cook mozzarella sticks, crab rangoons, mussels, shrimp, etc. Woks are also great for any floating items, such as donuts or funnel cake. When it comes to bigger things, such as chicken breasts, the shape is not as advantageous. You cannot fit more than a couple of them at once. Otherwise, they will slide down into one another, which will prevent them from frying evenly. How To Deep Fry in a Wok To achieve the best results with your cookware, get a deep frying or candy thermometer. You will need to measure the temperature of the oil before placing the ingredients inside. Follow the steps below: Pour some oil with a high smoke point into the wok (check our tips for the optimal depth below). Put your thermometer into the oil and wait until it heats up to 375 F. Start dropping the ingredients inside gradually. You can use wooden chopsticks or a metal skimmer. Be careful to prevent huge splashes. Keep stirring the ingredients until they turn golden brown. Use a slotted spoon or a spider strainer to remove the food from the oil, and dry it on paper towels. How Much Oil to Put in a Wok for Deep Frying? Aside from using only high smoke point oils, you need to measure the amount correctly. The rule of thumb is to never fill over 50% of the wok. Before frying any ingredients, dry them thoroughly to remove excess moisture. Add the pieces one at a time, so they fry more evenly. Conclusion Woks are great for a variety of techniques, but deep frying is their less obvious talent. Take advantage of the sloped shape to cook delicious meals quickly and easily, with less mess in the kitchen. At intense temperatures, using a wok for deep frying made of blue carbon steel is the best choice.
- [Carbon Steel vs. Cast Iron Pans: Let's Compare Them](https://us.yosukata.com/blogs/articles/carbon-steel-vs-cast-iron-pans-lets-compare-them): When it comes to frying, sautéing, or any other cooking method involving a pan, its material is just as important as the ingredients. Both carbon steel and cast iron are known for their durability, as they can serve your family for decades. At the same time, they also have some significant differences. Our comparison will help you choose between carbon steel vs. cast iron. Key Things to Know About Carbon Steel Pan vs Cast Iron These materials have a very similar composition. They both contain around 98-99% of iron and 1-2% of carbon. Nevertheless, their behavior on the stovetop is very different. These pans do not retain heat equally, which predisposes them to different cooking methods. Top Four Similarities Carbon steel and cast iron are similar materials with comparable limitations, functionality, and care requirements, including seasoning. These are multifunctional items that will always come in handy, even if you decide to radically modernize your kitchen. Here are the most notable similarities of carbon steel pan vs cast iron: Versatility You can use pans from either material for baking and frying, as they work equally well on a stovetop and in an oven. For example, if you buy a carbon steel skillet, you can sauté vegetables or cook some pizza in the oven (if you remove the wooden handle). Both types of kitchenware are suitable for induction stovetops thanks to the ferromagnetic metals they contain. Not Acid-Resistant Any sauces that contain acids break down the seasoning on carbon steel and cast iron pans. This happens because the acids react with their surfaces. Seasoning Is Required Here, carbon steel skillet vs cast iron are not very different, either, as they require coating with oil or fat. This process is known as seasoning, and the purpose is to make the surface virtually stick-free. As the oil penetrates the pores of the metal, it makes it less reactive. Careful Washing You should never wash these pans in a dishwasher or use any aggressive detergents. Otherwise, the seasoning will break down. Experts recommend washing with hot water using a scouring pad and only mild soap when it is absolutely necessary. Top Four Differences Between Carbon Steel vs Cast Iron Wok Despite their similarities, these materials have different heat retention properties, which determines the cooking instructions. You should not expect them to be equally durable, either. Discover the main distinctions of cast iron vs carbon steel below. Lifespan Cast iron is generally slightly less durable. This is due to its heavy fabrication. It makes it relatively brittle, so you can shatter or fracture these pans if you drop them. This is the first major advantage of carbon steel vs cast iron wok. Surface: Cast Iron vs Carbon Steel Properties Cast iron pans, as the term suggests, are formed in a cast. This explains why their surfaces have larger pores, and they are generally uneven. Thanks to higher malleability, carbon steel sheets are either pressed or hand-formed. As a result, they have a smoother surface and sides, which is another advantage of cast iron vs carbon steel wok. Weight Cast iron cookware is the heaviest. Carbon steel, on the other hand, is closer to stainless steel in terms of maneuverability. It is thinner. Heat Retention: A Big Cast Iron Carbon Steel Difference Cast iron has excellent heat retention properties. It warms up and cools down more slowly than carbon steel, but maintains the heat for a longer time. Carbon steel, on the other hand, is more suitable for delicate ingredients. It does retain heat quite well, but it also reacts to changes in the temperature faster. Cooking Both types of cookware perform well in the oven, but very differently on the stovetop. Cast iron is best for grilling and any dishes requiring aggressive heat. On the other hand, stir-frying is easier with carbon steel, as its quicker cooling prevents burning. In addition, as the surface is more even, it is more suitable for delicate meals like scallops. Conclusion: Carbon Steel Skillet vs Cast Iron As you can see, carbon steel has quite a few advantages, as it is lighter, smoother, and more durable. Yet, whether to use carbon steel or cast iron cookware is up to you. Adding new cookware to your arsenal is always a good thing!
- [How to Clean a Carbon Steel Wok: a Complete Guide](https://us.yosukata.com/blogs/articles/how-to-clean-a-carbon-steel-wok-a-complete-guide): Every wok owner should know how to clean a wok. A proper cleaning of your wok is what will help it serve you longer. If you do it after every meal preparation, it will have the best effect on the pan. It doesn’t matter what you were cooking, cleaning a wok is essential. Moreover, it prepares the utensil for the next use. In this article, we will tell you how to clean a carbon steel wok. The whole cleaning process is not difficult at all. Just make it your habit and your wok will be in the best condition for a long time. If you are frying in a wok frequently, our tip is to purchase a non-metallic scrubber in order to make the cleaning process even easier. How to Clean a Wok The wok has become extremely popular in the last years because it lets you cook delicious meals easily. The shape of the pan helps to distribute and concentrate the heat and many people even make sauces in it. But sediment from the oil and many Asian sauces that you might use, can gradually reduce cooking efficiency. In that way, it is essential to keep the inside walls of the wok clean. It is also better not to use strong chemicals. Therefore, knowing how to clean a wok is essential to preserve its qualities. Let’s find out how to clean a wok after cooking. There are two main methods. Let’s start with the traditional and the most popular one: Step 1 To clean a wok, first heat it over high heat. When a thick layer of oily sediment forms, place the pan over a strong fire and hold it until it gets very hot. Depending on the intensity of the heat, the wok can change color, becoming almost red in the middle. It may also slightly smoke and smell like metal. Step 2 Chip off the sediment. When the sediment starts to build up a crust (it really will be crunchy), the wok is ready to be cleaned. Turn off the heat. Be careful and hold the handle of the pan with a potholder and peel off the crust with a flat non-metal spatula, allowing the parts to fall into the center of the pan. Step 3 Cool the wok under running water. When you've removed the sediment as much as possible, rinse the pan under cold running water. Then use a natural bristle brush to brush off any remaining crust. You are now ready to start cooking again! Please note, that after such a thorough cleaning process, it is essential to season your wok with oil for further cooking. The second method is perfect for heavily dirty woks and you can choose any of them depending on the situation. Pour about 1.5 liters of water into a wok and heat it until it boils. Add about 40 grams of citric acid to the boiling water. Stir the liquid using a spatula and then scrub the pan with a sponge (still using a spatula or tongs) to clean the pan while it is still heating. Use a sponge and detergent to wash your wok in warm water. Wipe it dry with a paper towel after washing. How to Clean a Wok with Rust Woks can be made from different materials and some of them can easily rust. It can happen with your pan, too. Therefore, it requires constant maintenance – checking for rust, thorough drying, seasoning with a thin layer of oil. A good non-stick layer also helps to deal with this problem. Due to the tendency of carbon steel wok to rust, it cannot be soaked for a long time. The choice of the product to use for a rusty wok depends on how deep the rust is in the pan. As a rule, rust can be removed without any problems with a metal washcloth or brush. After cleaning, the pan must be rinsed well with water and dried with a towel, then season the pan with oil. Here is a simple instruction on how to get rid of rust: Pour about 1.5 liters of water in a wok placed over medium heat. Boil the water and add 40 grams of citric acid. While still being on heat, use a sponge, and, with the help of a spatula or tongs, clean the wok. It may take several minutes depending on the state of your wok. Now you can use a sponge and detergent to wash the pan in warm water. Pre-season the dry wok over the high heat. Wait for the bottom to turn blue and rotate the pan to pre-season the sides. Turn the heat off and wait for 5-7 minutes for the wok to cool down. Wash the wok again using a sponge and detergent. Carefully wipe the pan dry with a paper towel. Now you need to season your wok. Place it over medium heat and add about 2 tablespoons of vegetable oil. Wait for it to smoke slightly. Spread the oil evenly over the whole surface with the help of a paper towel and spatula. Do it for several minutes with the heat still on. Now wash the wok in warm water with a sponge but don’t use a detergent now. Wipe the wok dry and it is ready for cooking again. How to Wash a Wok The most important thing for cleaning a wok is to wait until the pan has cooled down and only then start washing. The temperature drop has a bad effect on any material. It is better to wait 10 minutes than spoil the utensil. You can wash the wok at a temperature convenient for you with the use of detergent and a soft sponge. If the food is stuck, use a metal or wooden...
